    "topic": "Graph",
    "type": "code",
    "prompt": "One idea discussed in lectures is that if we perform a graph traversal and take all the edges\nwere used during the traversal (i.e., edges that led to an unvisited vertex during the\ntraversal), these edges produce a spanning tree.\nFor example, consider the following graph:\nIf a breadth-first search was performed on the graph starting from vertex 0, then the edges\nused during the BFS would produce the following spanning tree:\nOne way to identify these edges is to use the predecessor array from the graph traversal,\nsince the example, edges in the spanning tree simply connect each vertex to its predecessor. For\nthe predecessor array below was produced from a BFS on the above from vertex 0. You can check it against the spanning tree shown above.\ngraph starting\nIndex 0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8\n- 0 0 0 2 1 3 5 6\nYour task is to implement breadth-first search and then return the spanning tree produced by\nthe BFS as a graph. Implement this function in bfsTree.c:\nGraph bfsTree(Graph g, int src);\nThis function takes a graph and a vertex src, and returns a new graph which is the spanning\ntree produced by a BFS in the graph starting from src. The BFS should prioritise lower-\nnumbered vertices, so if a vertex has multiple neighbours, they should be enqueued in\nincreasing order.\nYou can assume that the graph is connected and contains at least two vertices.\nFor this task, you must use the adjacency matrix representation, which is defined in\nGraphAdjMatrixRep.h.\nHere is the pseudocode for BFS:\nbfs(g, src):\nInputs: graph g\nsource vertex src\ninitialise visited and predecessor arrays\ncreate queue and enqueue src\nmark src as visited\nwhile the queue is not empty:\ndequeue u\nfor each neighbour v of u:\nif v has not been visited:\nmark v as visited\nset predecessor of v to u\nenqueue v\nHint: When creating the new spanning tree graph, you can use GraphInsertEdge to\ninsert edges.\nTesting\nOnce you think you've got the function working, test it by recompiling with make and running\nthe bfsTree program. The program takes two command-line arguments: the path to a\ngraph file, and a source vertex. For example:\n$ make\n...\n$ ./bfsTree graphs/graph-1.txt 0\n-----\nGraph\n-----\nNumber of vertices: 9\nEdges:\n0: 1 2 3\n1: 0 3 5\n2: 0 3 4\n3: 0 1 2 6\n4: 2\n5: 1 7\n6: 3 7 8\n7: 5 6\n8: 6\n----------------------\nBFS tree from vertex 0\n----------------------\nNumber of vertices: 9\nEdges:\n0: 1 2 3\n1: 0 5\n2: 0 4\n3: 0 6\n4: 2\n5: 1 7\n6: 3 8\n7: 5\n8: 6\nYou can check whether the output is correct by using the graph visualiser tool. First, switch\nto 2-graph mode, then copy the input graph into the first panel, and then copy the output\ngraph into the second panel. After moving the vertices around, you should get the following:",
