    "type": "code",
    "prompt": "Similarly, graph traversal with depth-first search also produces a spanning tree.\nFor example, depth-first search on the graph shown in Task 1 would produce the following\nspanning tree (assuming that the DFS prioritises lower-numbered vertices):\nYour task is to implement recursive depth-first search and then return the spanning tree\nproduced by the DFS as a graph. Implement this function in dfsTree.c:\nGraph dfsTree(Graph g, int src);\nThis function takes a graph and a vertex src, and returns a new graph which is the spanning\ntree produced by a DFS in the graph starting from src. The DFS should prioritise lower-\nnumbered vertices, so if a vertex has multiple neighbours, the neighbour with the lowest\nnumber should be visited first.\nYou can assume that the graph is connected and contains at least two vertices.\nFor this task, you must use the adjacency list representation, which is defined in\nGraphAdjListRep.h.\nHere is the pseudocode for DFS:\ndfs(g, src):\nInputs: graph g\nsource vertex src\ninitialise visited and predecessor arrays\nrecursiveDfs(g, src, visited, predecessor)\nrecursiveDfs(g, u, visited, predecessor):\nmark u as visited\nfor each neighbour v of u:\nif v has not been visited:\nset predecessor of v to u\nrecursiveDfs(g, v, visited, predecessor)\nTesting\nOnce you think you've got the function working, test it by recompiling with make and running\nthe dfsTree program. The program takes two command-line arguments: the path to a\ngraph file, and a source vertex. For example:\n$ make\n...\n$ ./dfsTree graphs/graph-1.txt 0\n-----\nGraph\n-----\nNumber of vertices: 9\nEdges:\n0: 1 2 3\n1: 0 3 5\n2: 0 3 4\n3: 0 1 2 6\n4: 2\n5: 1 7\n6: 3 7 8\n7: 5 6\n8: 6\n----------------------\nDFS tree from vertex 0\n----------------------\nNumber of vertices: 9\nEdges:\n0: 1\n1: 0 3\n2: 3 4\n3: 1 2 6\n4: 2\n5: 7\n6: 3 7 8\n7: 5 6\n8: 6\nAs in the previous task, you can check whether the output is correct by using the graph\nvisualiser tool.\nIntermission\nThe remaining tasks in this lab involve solving graph problems, where you will need to decide\nwhich graph traversal method, BFS or DFS, is more suitable, and then use your chosen\nmethod to solve the problem. This means you may be able to reuse some of your code from\nTasks 1 and 2.\nNote that a Queue ADT will be available in each task, but this does not necessarily mean that\nit is needed in every task. Also note that the Stack ADT will not be available - if you choose to\nuse DFS, you will need to implement it recursively.",
